---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What does DMARC do?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'DMARC tells receiving mail servers what to do when a message claiming to be from your domain fails SPF and DKIM checks, monitor, quarantine, or reject, and asks them to report the results back to you.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is a good DMARC policy?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Start at p=none with reporting to see who sends as your domain, fix legitimate senders, then move to p=quarantine and finally p=reject for full enforcement.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Where does a DMARC record live?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'In DNS, as a TXT record at _dmarc.yourdomain.com, note the leading reinforce.' } },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout title="DMARC: The Complete Guide" description="DMARC explained: policies (none, quarantine, reject), alignment, aggregate reports, and the safe road from monitoring to full enforcement." schema={[faq]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DMARC</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DMARC: The Complete Guide</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      SPF and DKIM verify mail, but neither tells the receiver <em>what to do</em> when verification
      fails. DMARC is the policy layer: it sets the consequence, requires alignment, and, essentially,
      reports back to you.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-three-policies">The three policies</a></li>
        <li><a href="#alignment-the-dmarc-twist">Alignment: the DMARC twist</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reports-the-part-nobody-skips-twice">Reports: the part nobody skips twice</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-safe-road-to-enforcement">The safe road to enforcement</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <Diagram name="dmarc-tree" caption="The DMARC decision tree: SPF/DKIM pass, alignment, policy" />

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="the-three-policies">The three policies</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><code>p=none</code>, monitor. Deliver the mail anyway, but send the domain owner aggregate reports.</li>
        <li><code>p=quarantine</code>, accept failures but junk them (spam folder). Use <code>pct</code> to ramp: 25% → 50% → 100%.</li>
        <li><code>p=reject</code>, refuse failures outright. Full enforcement; the end state.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="alignment-the-dmarc-twist">Alignment: the DMARC twist</h2>
      <p>
        Passing SPF alone isn't enough, the <strong>From:</strong> domain must also <em>align</em>
        with the SPF/DKIM domains. Relaxed alignment (the default) accepts matches on the
        organizational domain (<code>news.mail.example.com</code> aligns with <code>example.com</code>);
        strict requires exact matches. Alignment is why mail sent through an ESP fails DMARC until
        you configure the ESP to sign with your domain.
      </p>

      <h2 id="reports-the-part-nobody-skips-twice">Reports: the part nobody skips twice</h2>
      <p>
        With a <code>rua</code> address, every participating mailbox provider sends you daily XML
        summaries: every message using your domain, its source IP, SPF/DKIM results, and the action
        taken. This is how you discover the marketing SaaS sending as you, and the spoofers you
        never knew about. Read them with the free
        <a href="/dmarc-report-analyzer/">DMARC report analyzer</a>, or let
        <a href="/pricing/">monitoring</a> parse them continuously.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-safe-road-to-enforcement">The safe road to enforcement</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Publish <code>p=none</code> + <code>rua</code> (use the <a href="/dmarc-record-generator/">generator</a>).</li>
        <li>Read reports for 2-4 weeks; fix every legitimate sender (add to SPF, enable DKIM signing).</li>
        <li>Step to <code>p=quarantine; pct=25</code>, watch, ramp to 100%.</li>
        <li>Move to <code>p=reject</code>. Verify with the <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/">DMARC checker</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&\::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What does DMARC do?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DMARC tells receiving mail servers what to do when a message claiming to be from your domain fails SPF and DKIM checks, monitor, quarantine, or reject, and asks them to report the results back to you.</p></details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&\::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is a good DMARC policy?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Start at p=none with reporting to see who sends as your domain, fix legitimate senders, then move to p=quarantine and finally p=reject for full enforcement.</p></details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&\::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Where does a DMARC record live?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">In DNS, as a TXT record at _dmarc.yourdomain.com, note the leading reinforce.</p></details>
      </div>      <h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/spf-record/">SPF explained</a> · <a href="/dkim/">DKIM explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc-record/">DMARC record syntax reference</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
